✅ 3. Print Thread Names and IDs

3.1 Definition

Every thread created by the JVM has:

🔴 Thread Name → A human-readable identifier.
🔴 Thread ID → A unique numeric identifier assigned by the JVM.

These help identify which thread is executing a particular task.

3.2 Why Do We Need Thread Names and IDs?
🔴 Imagine a banking application where 100 threads process transactions simultaneously.

Without thread information, logs might look like:

Processing Payment...
Payment Successful...
Updating Balance...

It's impossible to know which thread performed which operation.

With thread information:

Thread-1 (ID: 12) -> Processing Payment
Thread-2 (ID: 13) -> Updating Balance
Thread-3 (ID: 14) -> Payment Successful

Now debugging becomes much easier.

3.3 Important Methods

| Method                   | Description                            |
| ------------------------ | -------------------------------------- |
| `Thread.currentThread()` | Returns the currently executing thread |
| `getName()`              | Returns the thread name                |
| `setName(String name)`   | Changes the thread name                |
| `getId()`                | Returns the unique thread ID           |


🔴 Thread.currentThread()
Returns a reference to the currently executing thread.

Syntax:
Thread.currentThread();

Example:
Thread current = Thread.currentThread();

🔴 getName()
Returns the name of the current thread.

Example:
System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName());

Output:
main

🔴 getId()
Returns the unique ID assigned to the thread.

Example:
System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getId());

Output:
1

3.4 Default Thread Names

When you don't specify a name:

Thread t1 = new Thread();
Thread t2 = new Thread();
Thread t3 = new Thread();

Possible names:

Thread-0
Thread-1
Thread-2

The JVM assigns default names automatically.

3.5 Setting Custom Thread Names

You can give meaningful names.

🔴 Method 1: Constructor
Thread t = new Thread(task, "Payment-Thread");

🔴 Method 2: setName()
t.setName("Payment-Thread");
